<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>An AI software company sold to enterprises in Europe, the United States and the Gulf. Its products used large language models for summarisation, classification and agent-style automation. Every serious deal now came with a security questionnaire, an AI questionnaire and often a data protection review. The questions overlapped heavily but never matched exactly, so the CTO and a few senior engineers answered them by hand, deal after deal.</p>
<p>Meanwhile the rules kept moving. The EU AI Act's obligations were phasing in, then partly delayed; US buyers asked about SOC 2 and the NIST AI framework; Gulf buyers asked about data residency and local regulation. The company needed one answer, not three.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>One inventory</strong> of every AI system and feature, with owner, purpose, models, data and risk rating.</li>
<li><strong>One control set</strong>, mapped to each framework, rather than a programme per regulation.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence by design</strong>: logs, evaluations and approvals produced by the delivery pipeline, not gathered by hand.</li>
<li><strong>Proportionate</strong>: governance effort matched to risk, so low-risk features ship without ceremony.</li>
<li><strong>Owned by engineering and product</strong>, with legal and security as partners rather than gatekeepers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Frameworks</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>EU AI Act<em>roles and obligations</em></span><span>ISO/IEC 42001<em>management system</em></span><span>NIST AI RMF<em>govern, map, measure, manage</em></span><span>SOC 2<em>trust services criteria</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Mapping</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Unified control set<em>one control, many mappings</em></span><span>Policy library<em>short, versioned</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Operate</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">AI system inventory<em>owner, purpose, risk</em></span><span>Risk review<em>proportionate tiers</em></span><span>Literacy programme<em>role-based</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Engineer</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Evaluation gates<em>quality and safety</em></span><span>Lineage<em>models, prompts, data</em></span><span>Access and logging<em>policy as code</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Evidence</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Evidence store<em>generated, timestamped</em></span><span>Trust centre<em>for buyers</em></span><span>Audit packs<em>for assessors</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Many frameworks on top, one control set in the middle, and evidence generated by the systems engineers already use.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Register</b><span>New AI feature added to the inventory with purpose, data and model</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Classify</b><span>Risk tier and applicable obligations assigned, including EU AI Act role</span></li>
<li><b>Build</b><span>Controls for the tier applied through templates and pipelines</span></li>
<li><b>Evaluate</b><span>Quality and safety evaluations run as release gates</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Evidence</b><span>Pipeline outputs stored as timestamped evidence against controls</span></li>
<li><b>Answer</b><span>Questionnaires and audits answered from the evidence store</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Governance moves with the delivery pipeline, so evidence exists before anyone asks for it.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The frameworks fit together better than their language suggests. The NIST AI Risk Management Framework organises AI risk work into govern, map, measure and manage functions<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. ISO/IEC 42001 specifies a certifiable AI management system<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. SOC 2 reports on controls against the trust services criteria for service organisations<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. One control can satisfy all three when it is written once and mapped carefully, as discussed in <a href="/insights/iso-42001-vs-nist-ai-rmf/">ISO 42001 vs the NIST AI RMF</a> and <a href="/insights/soc2-for-ai-startups/">SOC 2 for AI startups</a>.</p>

<h2>The hard part: a moving regulatory timeline</h2>
<p>The EU AI Act applies different obligations at different times<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. AI literacy duties already applied<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>, transparency obligations under Article 50 applied from 2 August 2026, and, as reported after the Digital Omnibus, stand-alone high-risk obligations moved to December 2027 with Annex I products following in August 2028<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Building for a single deadline would have meant rework every time it moved.</p>
<ul>
<li>Obligations were attached to inventory entries by role and use case, not to a calendar, so a date change updated a field, not a plan.</li>
<li>Controls that are valuable regardless of deadlines, such as inventory, evaluation, logging and human oversight, were built first.</li>
<li>High-risk-specific controls were designed early but switched on per product as classification and timelines firmed up.</li>
<li>Each EU AI Act question was tracked as provider or deployer, because the obligations differ.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><p>Build governance around capabilities, not deadlines. Inventories, evaluations and logs are useful under every regime; dates change, capabilities stay.</p></div>
<p>The background is in <a href="/insights/eu-ai-act-digital-omnibus-roadmap/">the Digital Omnibus roadmap</a>, <a href="/insights/eu-ai-act-gpai-fine-tuning-provider/">when fine-tuning makes you a provider</a> and, for Gulf buyers, <a href="/insights/uae-federal-authority-ai-data/">the UAE's Federal Authority for AI and Data</a>.</p>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Engineering</small><ol><li>Inventory and lineage</li><li class="hl">Evaluation and logging gates</li><li>Evidence automation</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Product</small><ol><li>Purpose and risk inputs</li><li>Human oversight design</li><li>User-facing transparency</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Legal and security</small><ol><li>Framework mapping</li><li class="hl">Policy and roles</li><li>Audit and certification</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Each team owns the evidence it naturally produces; nobody owns "doing compliance" alone.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Inventory</b><ul><li>Every AI feature listed</li><li>Owners and purposes</li><li>Initial risk tiers</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Control set</b><ul><li>Unified controls</li><li>Framework mappings</li><li>Short policies</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Automate</b><ul><li>Eval and logging gates</li><li>Evidence store</li><li>Trust centre</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Assure</b><ul><li>SOC 2 audit</li><li>ISO 42001 readiness</li><li>Quarterly regulatory review</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Inventory first: it is impossible to govern AI systems nobody has listed.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>One control set, many mappings</td><td>A programme per framework</td><td>Less duplication; new rules become mapping updates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Evidence from pipelines</td><td>Evidence gathered for each audit</td><td>Always current, cheaper, and more credible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Proportionate risk tiers</td><td>Same process for every feature</td><td>Keeps low-risk delivery fast and attention on what matters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Capabilities before deadlines</td><td>Plan around one regulatory date</td><td>Survives timeline changes without rework</td></tr>
<tr><td>Public trust centre</td><td>Answer every questionnaire from scratch</td><td>Buyers self-serve the common answers</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: start with the inventory and let engineering pipelines produce the evidence. Differently: involve sales engineering from the start. They see every questionnaire, and their list of repeated questions is the fastest way to prioritise which controls and evidence to build first.</p>
<p>If AI questionnaires are slowing your deals, my <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> work covers governance programmes like this, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
